# Expected measurements for nasty.txt, one row per line.
#
#   line  bytes  utf16  cells  graphemes
#
# Why this file exists: `codediff debug measure` shows cell widths, which a
# human can check against what the terminal actually draws. It cannot show byte
# or UTF-16 offsets — those are invisible on screen, and they are the ones the
# diff engine depends on. This table covers them.
#
# Provenance of each column, since "recorded from output" would make this a
# change detector rather than a check:
#
#   bytes, utf16,        cross-checked against Node's ICU `Intl.Segmenter`,
#   graphemes            `Buffer.byteLength` and `String.length` — a different
#                        Unicode implementation from the `unicode-segmentation`
#                        crate under test. All 14 rows agree exactly, including
#                        the ZWJ family (50) and the regional indicator pair
#                        (25).
#
#   cells                derived by hand from tab-stop arithmetic and East
#                        Asian Width, since no second implementation was
#                        available. Line 13 is the worked example: "tab" ends
#                        at column 3, so its tab advances 1 column to 4, while
#                        the next starts at 8 and advances a full 4.
#
# The interesting segmentation cases are pinned separately by unit tests in
# tests/coordinates.rs: a regional indicator pair is one cluster, an emoji ZWJ
# sequence is one cluster, and a combining mark does not start a new one.
#
# Line 9 holds the same word twice, precomposed (U+00E9) and decomposed
# (U+0065 U+0301). They look identical and measure identically in cells, yet
# differ in bytes and UTF-16 units — which is the whole point of the file.
#
# Line 11 — the emoji ZWJ family sequence — is a deliberate choice rather than
# a measurement. Unicode TR51 says it renders as a single glyph, so it is 2
# cells; counting its three component emoji separately gives 6, and some
# terminals do exactly that. If this row ever needs changing, the fix belongs
# in a per-terminal width override rather than in the measurement itself.

1    47   47   47   47
2    17   17   17   17
3    23   23   26   23
4    26   26   32   26
5    27   27   30   27
6    55   37   45   37
7    34   30   32   30
8    28   24   24   22
9    57   53   52   52
10   47   46   45   45
11   69   57   51   50
12   32   28   26   25
13   31   25   36   24
14  145  145  145  145
